      "commentary": "Two short sentences, and one of them is among the four \"great sayings\" the tradition singled out as containing the whole of its wisdom: ayam ātmā brahma — this Self is Brahman. Not a self somewhere else, not the Self of saints and sages only — this one, the very awareness reading this line, is identical with the ground of all reality. The Mandukya states it flatly, without argument, and then does something no other scripture quite does: it offers a method to verify it.\n\nThe method is in the second sentence. This Self has four quarters — catuṣpāt, literally \"four-footed.\" The word is deliberately concrete, almost humble; a quarter, a foot, a pāda, is just one part of something you can actually inspect. The Mandukya will not ask you to believe in Brahman. It will ask you to look, carefully, at the only four conditions consciousness is ever in — waking, dreaming, deep sleep, and a fourth — and to notice what is common to all of them. The grandest claim in Indian thought is about to be walked back to the most intimate possible evidence: your own nights and days. Everything you have ever experienced happened in one of these four. Examine them, and you examine yourself. Examine the Self, the verse promises, and you have found Brahman."
