      "commentary": "The survey begins where you are right now: the waking state, and the self you take yourself to be within it. Vaishvanara — \"the one common to all people\" — is awareness with its attention turned outward, bahiṣ-prajña, spending itself entirely on the world of solid objects, the sthūla, the gross. This is the self almost everyone means when they say \"I\": the one who wakes, works, wants, and meets a world of hard edges outside itself.\n\nThe archaic details — seven limbs, nineteen mouths — are a map of that engagement: the many faculties (the five senses, the five organs of action, the five vital breaths, and the four aspects of mind) through which waking consciousness reaches out and takes the world in. Don't get lost in the enumeration; catch the point it is quietly making. Even the waking self you are so sure of is not a simple thing but a busy apparatus of nineteen gates, all facing outward. It is the most public, most shared, most consensus-confirmed of the states — which is exactly why we mistake it for the whole of who we are. The Mandukya names it first not because it is deepest but because it is where the forgetting is thickest. This is the self we will spend the rest of the text learning to see through, beginning, in the next verse, with the discovery that consciousness can build an entire convincing world out of nothing but itself."
