      "commentary": "The second quarter is the dream, and it is placed here for a reason that ordinary life never pauses to consider. In dream, awareness turns inward — antaḥ-prajña — and does something astonishing: with no external light, no outside world, no sense-organs receiving anything, it constructs a complete universe. Landscapes, other people, weather, fear, delight, a whole convincing reality — all woven out of mind alone, out of the subtle, the pravivikta. And while the dream lasts, you do not doubt it for a second. It is utterly real, until you wake.\n\nThe name is Taijasa, \"the shining one,\" the self made of the mind's own light. And the Upanishad is using the dream as evidence, not decoration. Every night you personally demonstrate that consciousness is not merely a passive receiver of an outside world — it is a world-maker, capable of generating an entire realm, and of believing in it totally. The unsettling implication arrives on its own: if the mind can do that in dream, what exactly is it doing in waking? The next state deepens the vertigo, and the one after that resolves it. But already the neat certainty of verse three — the solid outer world, the solid self facing it — has been quietly destabilized. You have, it turns out, been a creator of worlds every night of your life, and forgotten it every morning."
