      "commentary": "The third quarter is the strangest, because it is the one everyone visits nightly and no one remembers: dreamless deep sleep. No desire pulls at you there; no dream flickers. The mind's endless production of images simply stops. And here the Mandukya says something a materialist account cannot easily explain: this state is prajñāna-ghana, a solid mass of pure awareness, and ānandamaya, made of bliss.\n\nConsider the evidence you already have. After true deep sleep you wake and say, without hesitation, \"I slept well; I knew nothing.\" Two remarkable claims in one breath. You were present enough to report on the state — someone was there — yet there was no content, no world, no division into subject and object, and what you bring back is not neutrality but a felt refreshment, a peace, a rest the whole restless day was secretly seeking. Everyone on earth drinks from this well every night. No one notices the water.\n\nThe Mandukya notices. It says: that nightly bliss is a clue. The peace you touch in dreamless sleep is a taste — muffled, unconscious, wrapped in a kind of darkness — of what you actually are underneath the traffic of waking and dreaming. Deep sleep is the mind at rest, and the rest feels like bliss because rest is your nature. But note the qualifier the next verses will insist on: it is bliss covered by ignorance, closeness without clarity. You are near the source here, but asleep to it. The task will be to find this same peace with the lights on."
