      "commentary": "Deep sleep, it turns out, is more than a nightly blank. This verse elevates Prajna to cosmic stature: the Lord of all, the knower of all, the inner controller — antaryāmin, the one seated within, running things from inside — and the yoni, the womb, from which all beings arise and into which they dissolve. Every night, in dreamless sleep, the entire world you carry simply folds back into an undivided source and, hours later, unfolds again. The Mandukya reads this small daily event as a miniature of the largest one: origin and dissolution, prabhava and apyaya, creation and return.\n\nWhy invest deep sleep with such grandeur? Because it is the causal state — the seed-condition in which the whole manifest world of waking and dreaming lies latent, unsprouted, held in potential the way an entire tree waits inside a seed. When you sleep dreamlessly, your personal world is not destroyed; it is withdrawn into its source, ready to bloom again at dawn. That is why you wake as the same person, your world reassembling around you in an instant.\n\nBut the tradition is careful, and so should we be: near is not the same as clear. Prajna is the closest of the three states to the truth — undivided, blissful, the source — yet it is a source wrapped in unknowing, a unity you sleep through rather than see. To mistake deep sleep's blank peace for liberation would be to mistake the seed for the harvest. The verse has walked us to the very edge of the source. The seventh verse takes the step the sleeper cannot: it opens the eyes without leaving the peace."
